original_form_data:
{"Address_address2":"Okhla Phase ‚Äì III","Email":"aali@childfundindia.org","Address_address1":"2nd Floor, Plot No 74","Website1":"https://in.linkedin.com/company/childfund-india","Website2":"https://x.com/ChildFundIndia","Number3":"2000","Formula":"60","Number2":"20000000","Number1":"2070000","MultiLine5":"We actively collaborate with government systems at both state and district levels to strengthen foundational learning and social-emotional learning (SEL) in schools. Our partnerships support the integration of FLN and SEL into classroom practices, teacher capacity-building, and community engagement efforts. We also work closely with State Commissions for Protection of Child Rights (SCPCR) to promote online safety for children through awareness campaigns and digital safety modules. In addition, we partner with government departments to enhance mental health support for children and adolescents by developing resources, training frontline workers, and improving access to psychosocial services. Together, these partnerships help embed large-scale, sustainable change within the public education and child protection systems.","SingleLine2":"ChildFund India","MultiLine7":"None","SingleLine3":"Sukhendu Santra","MultiLine1":"To create an India where children lead a dignified life and achieve their full potential.","SingleLine5":"Sr. Specialist- Education","MultiLine2":"Help deprived, excluded and vulnerable children to have the capacity to improve their conditions and gain opportunities to become responsible young adults, parents, leaders, etc., who would bring lasting and positive change in their communities.

Promote societies whose individuals and institutions participate in valuing, protecting and advancing the worth and the rights of children.

Enrich supporters‚Äô lives through their support of our cause.","MultiLine3":"ChildFund India works across key life stages of children, with Education as our core priority. We strengthen foundational learning through ECCE, FLN, and SEL, while advancing STEM, coding, and girls‚Äô higher education through innovative programs. Alongside education, we support children‚Äôs health and nutrition, strengthen community-based child protection systems, and empower youth and families through livelihoods and skilling initiatives. We also provide timely humanitarian response during crises, ensuring children grow up safe, healthy, and prepared for the future.","Address_zipCode":"110020","MultiLine4":"Our work has significantly strengthened education outcomes across 14 states and 123 districts in India. We reach over 2.7 million children annually under the Education thematic, achieving nearly 70% improvement in learning outcomes, around 65% growth in children‚Äôs social-emotional skills, and about 85% SEL capacity enhancement among teachers. Our STEM programs have boosted students‚Äô skills by approximately 85%, while 95‚Äì100% of participating teachers report improved STEM teaching competencies. Together, these efforts are transforming learning environments and improving children‚Äôs overall well-being and future readiness.","Number11":"","Number10":"40","Name_Last":"Ali","MultipleChoice":["India"],"SingleLine31":"","SingleLine32":"","Dropdown":"No","SubForm1":[{"firstName":"Anand","lastName":"Vishwakarma","email7":"AVishwakarma@ChildFund.org"},{"firstName":"Suresh","lastName":"Krishna","email7":"sureshkkrishna@gmail.com"}],"MultipleChoice2":["Curriculum design and development","Education technology","Learning outcomes assessments","Parent and community engagement","Remediation support","School development and improvement","Teacher development and certification","Teaching and learning material"],"MultipleChoice1":["Grade 12"],"MultipleChoice4":null,"MultipleChoice3":["Foundational literacy and numeracy","Girls education","Secondary education and work readiness","Social and emotional learning"],"Dropdown9":"Yes","Name5_First":"Anand","Name5_Last":"Vishwakarma","Dropdown4":"Yes, all of the countries","Dropdown3":"India","Dropdown2":"Non-profit","Dropdown1":"Yes","Dropdown8":"Poor (less than $2 a day)","Dropdown6":"","SingleLine":"Sr. Specialist- Education","Dropdown5":"No","Dropdown10":"Yes","Dropdown11":null,"Dropdown12":"Students","Dropdown13":null,"Radio2":"Yes","Radio1":"Yes","Dropdown15":"GSF Website","MonthYear":"Dec-1984","Website":"https://childfundindia.org/","Name_First":"Ashique","MultiLine":"","Name4_First":"Ashique","Name4_Last":"Ali","Number":"452","Email6":"ssantra@childfundindia.org","SubForm":[{"firstName":"Mahesh","lastName":"Patidar","jobTitle":"Program Manager","organisation":"CENTRE FOR ADVANCED RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT","email":"mahesh.patidar.card@gmail.com"},{"firstName":"Ashish","lastName":"Kumar","jobTitle":"Program Manager","organisation":"Vigyan Foundation","email":"chitrakootcdp@gmail.com"}],"Email1":"AVishwakarma@ChildFund.org","Email2":"aali@childfundindia.org","Address_country":"India","Address_city":"New Delhi"}

form_data:
{"Name_First":"Ashique","Name_Last":"Ali","SingleLine":"Sr. Specialist- Education","SingleLine2":"ChildFund India","Email":"aali@childfundindia.org","Website":"https://childfundindia.org/","Website1":"https://in.linkedin.com/company/childfund-india","Website2":"https://x.com/ChildFundIndia","Dropdown":"No","Name5_First":"Anand","Name5_Last":"Vishwakarma","Email1":"AVishwakarma@ChildFund.org","Dropdown1":"Yes","Name4_First":"Ashique","Name4_Last":"Ali","SingleLine5":"Sr. Specialist- Education","Email2":"aali@childfundindia.org","MonthYear":"Dec-1984","Dropdown2":"Non-profit","Dropdown3":"India","MultipleChoice":["India"],"Dropdown4":"Yes, all of the countries","MultiLine":"","MultiLine1":"To create an India where children lead a dignified life and achieve their full potential.","MultiLine2":"Help deprived, excluded and vulnerable children to have the capacity to improve their conditions and gain opportunities to become responsible young adults, parents, leaders, etc., who would bring lasting and positive change in their communities.

Promote societies whose individuals and institutions participate in valuing, protecting and advancing the worth and the rights of children.

Enrich supporters‚Äô lives through their support of our cause.","MultiLine3":"ChildFund India works across key life stages of children, with Education as our core priority. We strengthen foundational learning through ECCE, FLN, and SEL, while advancing STEM, coding, and girls‚Äô higher education through innovative programs. Alongside education, we support children‚Äôs health and nutrition, strengthen community-based child protection systems, and empower youth and families through livelihoods and skilling initiatives. We also provide timely humanitarian response during crises, ensuring children grow up safe, healthy, and prepared for the future.","Dropdown12":"Students","MultipleChoice1":["Grade 12"],"MultipleChoice2":["Curriculum design and development","Education technology","Learning outcomes assessments","Parent and community engagement","Remediation support","School development and improvement","Teacher development and certification","Teaching and learning material"],"MultipleChoice3":["Foundational literacy and numeracy","Girls education","Secondary education and work readiness","Social and emotional learning"],"Dropdown5":"No","Dropdown6":"Other","Number11":"","Number":"452","Number1":"2070000","Number2":"20000000","Number10":"40","Formula":"60","Number3":"2000","Dropdown8":"Poor (less than $2 a day)","MultiLine4":"Our work has significantly strengthened education outcomes across 14 states and 123 districts in India. We reach over 2.7 million children annually under the Education thematic, achieving nearly 70% improvement in learning outcomes, around 65% growth in children‚Äôs social-emotional skills, and about 85% SEL capacity enhancement among teachers. Our STEM programs have boosted students‚Äô skills by approximately 85%, while 95‚Äì100% of participating teachers report improved STEM teaching competencies. Together, these efforts are transforming learning environments and improving children‚Äôs overall well-being and future readiness.","SingleLine31":"ChildFund India uses a robust internal monitoring system known as the All India Management System (AIMS). This system captures programme logframes, targets, budgets, outputs, and outcomes prior to implementation. The organisation conducts baseline, midline, endline, and quarterly assessments to track progress, with a strong focus on learning outcomes in literacy, numeracy, and social-emotional learning.","SingleLine32":"Donor-funded projects are subject to mandatory third-party evaluations, with separate independent agencies engaged for baseline, midline, and endline assessments to minimise bias and ensure objectivity.","Dropdown9":"Yes","MultiLine5":"We actively collaborate with government systems at both state and district levels to strengthen foundational learning and social-emotional learning (SEL) in schools. Our partnerships support the integration of FLN and SEL into classroom practices, teacher capacity-building, and community engagement efforts. We also work closely with State Commissions for Protection of Child Rights (SCPCR) to promote online safety for children through awareness campaigns and digital safety modules. In addition, we partner with government departments to enhance mental health support for children and adolescents by developing resources, training frontline workers, and improving access to psychosocial services. Together, these partnerships help embed large-scale, sustainable change within the public education and child protection systems.","Dropdown10":"Yes","SingleLine3":"Sukhendu Santra","Email6":"ssantra@childfundindia.org","MultiLine7":"None","Dropdown15":"GSF Website","SubForm":[{"firstName":"Mahesh","lastName":"Patidar","jobTitle":"Program Manager","organisation":"CENTRE FOR ADVANCED RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT","email":"mahesh.patidar.card@gmail.com"},{"firstName":"Ashish","lastName":"Kumar","jobTitle":"Program Manager","organisation":"Vigyan Foundation","email":"chitrakootcdp@gmail.com"}],"Radio1":"Yes","Radio2":"Yes","SubForm1":[{"firstName":"Anand","lastName":"Vishwakarma","email7":"AVishwakarma@ChildFund.org"},{"firstName":"Suresh","lastName":"Krishna","email7":"sureshkkrishna@gmail.com"}],"SingleLine11":"ChildFund India was founded on 31 December 1984 by a group of nine founding members and registered as a legally compliant society in India. The organisation was established to improve the well-being of children and young people through long-term, community-led development interventions. With over four decades of continuous operation, ChildFund India has built deep-rooted community presence and institutional credibility across multiple regions in the country.","SingleLine12":"ChildFund India operates a diversified funding model that includes child sponsorships, corporate social responsibility (CSR) grants, government funding, and institutional and bilateral donor support. CSR funding is a significant source of revenue in India, particularly for education and health programmes, while government funding currently supports health interventions in select states. Institutional donors have included the European Union and Germany‚Äôs BMZ.","SingleLine13":"ChildFund India is governed by a Board comprising between eight and nine members, in line with Indian legal requirements for registered societies. The Board conducts annual internal elections to appoint the President, Secretary, and Treasurer. Board members are selected based on complementary expertise in areas such as education, health, livelihoods, evidence, and business development.","SingleLine14":"The organisation is led operationally by an Executive Director who oversees the country portfolio, supported by a Senior Management Team and a Country Leadership Team responsible for programme oversight and state-level operations.","SingleLine15":"The organisation operates across 12 states and 2 Union Territories in India.","SingleLine17":"ChildFund India works across five thematic areas: education, health, livelihoods and skills development, child protection and advocacy, and climate change, sustainability, and resilience. Education is the dominant focus area, accounting for approximately 60% of the organisation‚Äôs total programme budget. Education programmes include early childhood care and education, foundational literacy and numeracy, STEM and science education, coding and robotics, girls‚Äô education, career guidance and counselling, and adolescent girls‚Äô empowerment programmes aimed at preventing child marriage and supporting continued education. The organisation currently supports approximately 28,000 sponsored children, with programme benefits extending to entire communities. The majority of sponsored children fall within the 6‚Äì14 age group, which informs the strong emphasis on education programming. ChildFund India works with 452 schools, primarily government and government-aided institutions. Many school partnerships have been sustained for over two to three decades under the long-term sponsorship model, reflecting continuity and depth of engagement.","SingleLine28":"All programme designs and materials are aligned with India‚Äôs National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 and the National Curriculum Framework (NCF) revised in 2022 and 2023. Where programmes are implemented at state level, ChildFund India also aligns its interventions with the relevant State Curriculum Frameworks to ensure contextual relevance and compliance with government standards.","SingleLine29":"For foundational literacy and numeracy programmes, the average cost of intervention is approximately INR 150‚Äì160 per child per year, equivalent to about USD 2, depending on programme design and funding source.","MultiLine6":"Education interventions are implemented exclusively in government and government-aided schools.","SingleLine8":"School engagement follows two primary models. Under the sponsorship model, ChildFund India maintains long-term partnerships with schools and communities, often spanning several decades. For institutional and corporate donor-funded projects, engagement is typically time-bound to three or four years. In such cases, the organisation implements structured exit and sustainability plans, including strengthening school management committees and building government ownership, while maintaining light-touch follow-up through nearby sponsorship-supported programmes.","SingleLine33":"Key strengths of ChildFund India include its long-standing presence in communities for over 40 years, strong thematic expertise across multiple development sectors, and a life-stage approach that supports children and youth from early childhood through young adulthood. The organisation has well-established partnerships with government institutions at district and state levels and has developed mature internal systems for grant management, donor compliance, financial controls, risk management, and reporting.","SingleLine34":"The organisation operates in an increasingly complex regulatory environment, particularly with respect to foreign funding under India‚Äôs Foreign Contribution Regulation Act (FCRA). Additional challenges include short funding cycles associated with CSR and institutional grants, as well as bureaucratic delays that can affect fund disbursement and programme timelines. These challenges require ongoing adaptive management and strong compliance systems.","SingleLine35":"ChildFund India seeks to join the Global Schools Forum to strengthen technical collaboration, peer learning, and knowledge exchange in education. Membership is viewed as an opportunity to increase programme visibility, disseminate evidence-based practices, and co-create robust evidence on foundational literacy, numeracy, and social-emotional learning. The organisation also aims to diversify funding sources, scale proven education models, and contribute its extensive experience and data to global education dialogue and practice.","SingleLine36":"ChildFund India will contribute to the GSF community through active knowledge sharing, technical collaboration, and evidence generation. The organisation will share evidence-based education models, particularly in foundational literacy and numeracy and social-emotional learning, drawing on its large-scale and long-term programming experience in India. It will participate in piloting and refining innovative approaches with other members, contribute data and learning from robust monitoring systems and third-party evaluations, and support joint research initiatives. ChildFund India will also contribute to dissemination efforts by sharing case studies, tools, and lessons learned through GSF platforms and international forums, while leveraging its wider ChildFund network to provide comparative insights and scalable practices.","Number4":"160"}

